1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,912
Here are three hundred and ninety-eight cars:

2
00:00:02,912 --> 00:00:04,300
how heavy each one is,

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:05,738
and how far it goes on a gallon of

4
00:00:05,738 --> 00:00:06,625
fuel.

5
00:00:07,133 --> 00:00:10,908
Linear regression fits one straight line through this cloud.

6
00:00:10,908 --> 00:00:13,558
You have seen this equation a thousand times.

7
00:00:14,067 --> 00:00:16,417
But what is the line actually claiming?

8
00:00:16,417 --> 00:00:18,167
Not that every car sits on it.

9
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:19,742
They clearly do not.

10
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:21,288
Pick a weight.

11
00:00:21,288 --> 00:00:23,312
Say fifteen hundred kilograms.

12
00:00:23,312 --> 00:00:25,300
Here are the cars near that weight.

13
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:27,987
Average their fuel economy,

14
00:00:27,987 --> 00:00:29,487
and you get one number.

15
00:00:29,487 --> 00:00:31,462
That number is what the line is trying to

16
00:00:31,462 --> 00:00:32,225
be.

17
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:33,758
Slide the weight,

18
00:00:33,758 --> 00:00:35,721
and the averages trace a path.

19
00:00:35,721 --> 00:00:38,358
The line is the straight-line guess for that path:

20
00:00:38,358 --> 00:00:39,233
the mean of y,

21
00:00:39,233 --> 00:00:40,308
given x.

22
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:43,292
Close. Not exact.

23
00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:44,392
Remember that.

24
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:47,962
Now look at the cars near fifteen hundred again.

25
00:00:47,962 --> 00:00:50,538
Each one misses the line by some amount.

26
00:00:50,538 --> 00:00:52,900
Regression calls these residuals.

27
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,200
Their spread has a name too:

28
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,137
sigma. The textbook writes it as noise added to

29
00:00:58,137 --> 00:00:59,100
the line.

30
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:00,600
But that word,

31
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,300
noise, hides the point.

32
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:04,000
The spread is not an error.

33
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,800
It is part of the prediction.

34
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:07,850
Put the two together.

35
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:08,700
At each weight,

36
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,562
a centre from the line and a spread from

37
00:01:10,562 --> 00:01:12,800
sigma: that is a whole bell curve,

38
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,075
one per weight.

39
00:01:14,583 --> 00:01:17,908
Ordinary least squares assumes every bell has the same

40
00:01:17,908 --> 00:01:19,808
width. Slide along,

41
00:01:19,808 --> 00:01:22,033
and watch the real cars disagree:

42
00:01:22,033 --> 00:01:24,358
the spread shrinks as they get heavier.

43
00:01:27,283 --> 00:01:29,546
So the honest statement is this.

44
00:01:29,546 --> 00:01:32,108
Given x, y has a distribution.

45
00:01:32,108 --> 00:01:33,533
Its centre moves with x,

46
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:35,096
and so does its spread.

47
00:01:35,096 --> 00:01:37,408
The line was only ever the centre.

48
00:01:37,917 --> 00:01:40,542
Regression is not predicting a number.

49
00:01:40,542 --> 00:01:43,479
It is predicting a conditional distribution.

50
00:01:43,479 --> 00:01:46,367
Ordinary least squares is the special case where that

51
00:01:46,367 --> 00:01:47,979
distribution is Normal,

52
00:01:47,979 --> 00:01:49,642
its width is constant,

53
00:01:49,642 --> 00:01:51,742
and you only model the centre.

54
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,050
Everything that follows in this series is about dropping

55
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:56,563
those three assumptions,

56
00:01:56,563 --> 00:01:57,950
one at a time.
